{
  "id": "xnvyg2c",
  "title": "CHAPTER 9 : COMPOUND INTEREST",
  "order": 13,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "njelyb0",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "<strong>Introduction to Compound Interest</strong>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Chapter",
          "value": "Compound Interest",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Growth Model",
          "value": "Exponential",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Concept",
          "value": "Successive Rates",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Exam Priority",
          "value": "Very High",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "30z1pwu"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "94b4wm6",
      "content": "Compound Interest is the interest calculated on both the original principal and the accumulated interest from previous periods. This means that after every compounding period, the principal increases, causing the interest to grow at an accelerating rate."
    },
    {
      "id": "hc0zvql",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Mathematical Foundation of Compound Interest"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xrfgzjg",
      "content": "Let the principal be P and the annual rate of interest be R%."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7d4wb0q",
      "content": "After one year:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xeqe3ny",
      "content": "Interest = (P × R)/100"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "daim61d",
      "content": "Amount = P + (P × R)/100 = P(1 + R/100)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dttznrm",
      "content": "After two years:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "smfrevu",
      "content": "The new principal becomes P(1 + R/100). So the amount becomes:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zian6rc",
      "content": "P(1 + R/100)(1 + R/100) = P(1 + R/100)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pwkxoxx",
      "content": "After T years:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dzxh8m9",
      "content": "A = P(1 + R/100)^T"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lh9td7k",
      "content": "This clearly shows that compound interest follows exponential growth rather than linear growth."
    },
    {
      "id": "fggnbbz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Fundamental Terms"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6qajqjj",
      "content": "Principal (P): The original sum invested or borrowed. Rate (R): The percentage of interest charged per year. Time (T): Duration of investment (generally in years). Amount (A): Final value after interest."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4cexb3d",
      "content": "Compound Interest (CI): Amount minus Principal."
    },
    {
      "id": "57cg2bf",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "<strong>Most Important Formulas </strong>Amount: A = P(1 + R/100)^T Compound Interest: CI = A − P"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h6oc6rd",
      "content": "For different yearly rates: A = P(1+R1/100)(1+R2/100)(1+R3/100) Half-yearly compounding: Rate → R/2, Time → 2T"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "eyb2i1c",
      "content": "Quarterly compounding: Rate → R/4, Time → 4T"
    },
    {
      "id": "3zqwj1h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Understanding Exponential Growth"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pp1n465",
      "content": "In Simple Interest, the interest is constant every year. In Compound Interest, interest increases every year because the base value keeps growing. Even a small difference in rate produces a large difference over time."
    },
    {
      "id": "d9pjo7v",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "<strong>Growth Factor Approach </strong>Define the growth factor as (1 + R/100). If R = 10%, growth factor = 1.10."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zlw900r",
      "content": "So after 3 years:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "lb5zmoq",
      "content": "A = P × 1.1^3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2r4fsan",
      "content": "This method improves calculation speed and reduces mistakes."
    },
    {
      "id": "9llqq9a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Difference Between Simple Interest and Compound Interest"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hdehsx8",
      "content": "For the same principal, rate, and time:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0rp4byx",
      "content": "CI ≥ SI (except for one year). For 2 years:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "zszruxb",
      "content": "Difference = P(R/100)^2"
    },
    {
      "id": "vkumzlo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Binomial Expansion Insight (Advanced)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "oq9g26q",
      "content": "(1 + R/100)^2 = 1 + 2R/100 + (R/100)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lx11b85",
      "content": "The last term represents the extra interest generated due to compounding."
    },
    {
      "id": "nudimuu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Depreciation Model"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1o3t3pv",
      "content": "When value decreases annually, use:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "03st2y4",
      "content": "Value = P(1 − R/100)^T"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pc6hwqo",
      "content": "This is mathematically identical to compound interest with a negative rate."
    },
    {
      "id": "44l2bnh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Successive Rate Concept"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jide3s5",
      "content": "If rates change each year, multiply growth factors separately instead of averaging the rates."
    },
    {
      "id": "4ifps59",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Very Important Exam Tips"
    },
    {
      "id": "n8b49o2",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Always check the compounding period.</li><li>Simplify before multiplying.</li><li>Use squares when possible.</li><li>Do not confuse CI with SI.</li><li>Identify growth factor quickly.</li></ul>",
        "Always check the compounding period.",
        "Simplify before multiplying.",
        "Use squares when possible.",
        "Do not confuse CI with SI.",
        "Identify growth factor quickly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tih8isa",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Solved Examples"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5gw9o8b",
      "content": "Example 1: Find the compound interest on ₹10,000 at 10% per annum for 2 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qe66jwe",
      "content": "Step 1: Identify the given values. Principal (P) = ₹10,000"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bc48ina",
      "content": "Rate (R) = 10% per annum Time (T) = 2 years"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sc79hgi",
      "content": "Step 2: Write the compound amount formula. A = P(1 + R/100)^T"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "my9qmmm",
      "content": "Step 3: Substitute the values into the formula. A = 10000(1 + 10/100)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "e97f6hv",
      "content": "A = 10000(1.1)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "od0jn3s",
      "content": "Step 4: Calculate the power carefully."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qe9pxdl",
      "content": "1.1 × 1.1 = 1.21"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "irg98q0",
      "content": "Step 5: Multiply with the principal. Amount = 10000 × 1.21"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "cf15x4c",
      "content": "Amount = ₹12,100"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7dmeg72",
      "content": "Step 6: Find Compound Interest. CI = Amount − Principal"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6x7uk7z",
      "content": "CI = 12,100 − 10,000 CI = ₹2,100"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t7g3hld",
      "content": "Final Answer: The compound interest is ₹2,100."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jz5qi4k",
      "content": "Example 2: Find the amount on ₹8,000 at 5% per annum for 3 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vrxwuvn",
      "content": "Step 1: Identify the values. P = ₹8,000"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "4yiqlwl",
      "content": "R = 5%"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "etos1hx",
      "content": "T = 3 years"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d9xn0xw",
      "content": "Step 2: Use the amount formula. A = 8000(1 + 5/100)^3"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qf2cpj9",
      "content": "A = 8000(1.05)^3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6uwi9sb",
      "content": "Step 3: Calculate the power step-by-step to avoid mistakes. First square the value:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ffalvbq",
      "content": "1.05 × 1.05 = 1.1025"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wknb8kp",
      "content": "Now multiply once more:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2bmhglt",
      "content": "1.1025 × 1.05 = 1.157625"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wx7do9a",
      "content": "Step 4: Multiply with the principal. Amount = 8000 × 1.157625"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qf85aa7",
      "content": "Amount = 9261 (approximately) Step 5: Interpret the result."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lprevr2",
      "content": "This means the investment grows by ₹1,261 over three years. Final Answer: The amount is approximately ₹9,261."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "piwpb60",
      "content": "Example 3: Find the compound interest on ₹5,000 when the rate is 10% for the first year and 20% for the second year."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Simple Interest (SI)",
        "Compound Interest (CI)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Principal Basis",
          "Remains constant throughout the tenure",
          "Changes periodically as interest accumulates"
        ],
        [
          "Growth Pattern",
          "Linear growth",
          "Exponential growth"
        ],
        [
          "Interest Yield",
          "Lower returns over extended periods",
          "Higher returns due to interest on interest"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "an4oac6"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v0auipb",
      "content": "Step 1: When rates are different, calculate year-by-year instead of using a single formula. Principal = ₹5,000"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "den0bve",
      "content": "Step 2: Calculate the amount after the first year. Interest for Year 1 = 10% of 5000"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "yif7rzb",
      "content": "= 500"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6gq83b2",
      "content": "New Amount = 5000 + 500"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gnd0n26",
      "content": "= ₹5,500"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u2nyo9t",
      "content": "Step 3: This new amount becomes the principal for the second year. Interest for Year 2 = 20% of 5500"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "c04x9ca",
      "content": "= 1100"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ruzuk95",
      "content": "Final Amount = 5500 + 1100"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "jhm6lbv",
      "content": "= ₹6,600"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fvmk0cv",
      "content": "Step 4: Calculate Compound Interest. CI = Final Amount − Original Principal CI = 6600 − 5000"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8t7o16s",
      "content": "CI = ₹1,600"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fthcocx",
      "content": "Final Answer: The compound interest is ₹1,600."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "egpbyie",
      "content": "Example 4: Find the compound interest on ₹6,000 at 12% per annum compounded half-yearly for 1 year."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2y6s0qa",
      "content": "Step 1: Understand half-yearly compounding. Interest is calculated twice a year."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "k63lyj8",
      "content": "So,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c3nl64m",
      "content": "New Rate = 12% ÷ 2 = 6%"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6gbzeck",
      "content": "Number of periods = 1 × 2 = 2 Step 2: Apply the formula."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "77evuk7",
      "content": "A = 6000(1.06)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yz8v20f",
      "content": "Step 3: Calculate the square."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "e4kbbj5",
      "content": "1.06 × 1.06 = 1.1236"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b8z4j29",
      "content": "Step 4: Multiply with principal. Amount = 6000 × 1.1236"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "azokgjq",
      "content": "Amount = ₹6,741.6 Step 5: Calculate CI. CI = 6741.6 − 6000 CI = ₹741.6"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e6ky7lk",
      "content": "Final Answer: The compound interest is ₹741.6."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3qfpjpd",
      "content": "Example 5: Find the difference between Compound Interest and Simple Interest on ₹4,000 at 5% for 2 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xf4v67h",
      "content": "Step 1: Use the direct mathematical formula for difference. Difference = P(R/100)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x182qv6",
      "content": "Step 2: Substitute values. Difference = 4000(5/100)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xqawk9c",
      "content": "= 4000(0.05)^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yg4o4dq",
      "content": "Step 3: Square the value."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dly1v4k",
      "content": "0.05 × 0.05 = 0.0025"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Pay careful attention to compounding periods (annual, semi-annual, quarterly) to adjust the rate and time variables accordingly.",
        "Questions comparing Simple Interest and Compound Interest for 2 and 3-year periods are frequently tested in competitive exams.",
        "Remember that depreciation formulas use a negative rate factor (1 - R/100) instead of the standard addition factor."
      ],
      "id": "edo9kro"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xhk5wa9",
      "content": "Step 4: Multiply."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h6csuxg",
      "content": "Difference = 4000 × 0.0025 Difference = ₹10 Interpretation:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "omaws3k",
      "content": "Compound Interest exceeds Simple Interest by ₹10 because interest in the second year is calculated on the accumulated interest as well."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jt796dj",
      "content": "Final Answer: The difference is ₹10."
    },
    {
      "id": "klulpzp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Practice Questions with Detailed Solutions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f0ejexs",
      "content": "Question 1: Find CI on ₹7000 at 10% for 2 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6fr5j1i",
      "content": "A = 7000(1.1)^2 = 8470 CI = 1470."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "msv1uv1",
      "content": "Question 2: Find the amount on ₹12,000 at 6% for 2 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hya2h14",
      "content": "A = 12000(1.06)^2 = 13483.2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g6pgw9t",
      "content": "Question 3: Find CI when ₹9,000 is invested at 8% compounded half-yearly for 1 year."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "57ql1zi",
      "content": "A = 9000(1.04)^2 = 9734.4 CI = 734.4."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f5j0283",
      "content": "Question 4: A machine worth ₹50,000 depreciates at 10% annually for 2 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kd9n7mp",
      "content": "Value = 50000(0.9)^2 = 40500."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pqmg4qb",
      "content": "Question 5: Find the difference between CI and SI on ₹5,000 at 8% for 2 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0d5z2o4",
      "content": "Difference = 5000 × (0.08)^2 = 32."
    },
    {
      "id": "qz83ob5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "FINAL INSIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Compound interest calculates interest on both the principal amount and the accumulated interest over prior periods.",
        "Compound interest follows exponential growth, whereas simple interest exhibits linear growth over time.",
        "The depreciation model applies a negative compound rate to represent asset value loss over successive years.",
        "Successive rate concepts allow straightforward multi-year calculations when annual interest rates vary."
      ],
      "id": "9gj6c4l"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o8jdml2",
      "content": "Compound Interest is one of the most intelligence-driven chapters in arithmetic because it introduces exponential thinking. The most frequently tested areas include CI vs SI difference, successive rates, half-yearly compounding, and depreciation."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}